The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
rc = bnxt_qplib_free_mrw(&rdev->qplib_res, &mr->qplib_mr);
if (rc)
    kfree(mr);
```
Reason:
The code attempts to free the Memory Region (MR) structure `mr` if the deregistration process fails (indicated by a non-zero return code from `bnxt_qplib_free_mrw`). This leads to a situation where the MR is freed even when the operation did not complete successfully, resulting in access to an already freed memory location upon retrying the deregistration, causing a kernel NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (rc) {
    dev_err(rdev_to_dev(rdev), "Dereg MR failed: %#x\n", rc);
    return rc;
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a block that checks if the deregistration process returns an error (non-zero code). Instead of freeing the MR structure immediately, it logs an error message and returns the error code. This prevents the structure from being freed if the deregistration fails, ensuring that the MR remains valid for any future operations, thus avoiding crashes due to null pointer dereference when retrying the deregistration.

